  "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-2(x+2),\\,{t}^{2}=-4(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-2(x+2))}^{2} \\\\  & =4{(x+2)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4(y+3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+2)}^{2} & =-4(y+3) \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
